{"fingerprint":"2e85dc0dd9ec5fa8d64e0e6b04473bad218d80e201d2dca2a11580775dcf6557","path":"src/review_gauntlet/run_controller.py","rule_id":"test-evidence","content":"Post-loop finalization at lines 453-463 marks the run as `completed` and emits a `finalized` event but skips the checkpoint commit that the normal finalization path (lines 424-442) performs. If the session disappears exactly when `max_steps` is exhausted, any pending checkpoint data is silently dropped. Consider whether `_commit_finalized_checkpoint` should also be called here, or at least log/emit an event noting the checkpoint was skipped.","suggestion_code":"","existing_code":"        session_id = self._active_session_id_or_none()
        if session_id is None:
            self._mark_finalized()
            self._emit("finalized", session_id=None)
            return _run_result(
                completed=True,
                steps=steps,
                reason="completed",
                error=None,
                session_id=None,
            )","start_line":453,"end_line":463,"thinking":"The in-loop finalization path (lines 424-442) commits the checkpoint before marking finalized. The post-loop path at lines 453-463 only fires when the session vanishes between the last step and the loop exit, which is a narrow window, but it silently skips the checkpoint commit. If the last agent step produced checkpoint data, it would be lost. This may be intentional (no session = nothing to commit against) but the asymmetry is worth documenting or handling explicitly.","imprecise":false}

